# ============================================================
# Elisa core Runtime - JSON (RFC 8259 parser, DOM and serialiser)
# ============================================================
#
# A conservative, complete JSON subset: everything RFC 8259 requires and
# nothing more. The whole DOM is arena-allocated - one `region`/`Arena` owns
# every node, key and decoded string, so there is nothing to free and no
# ownership to thread. A `JsonValue` is valid exactly as long as the arena it
# was parsed into.
#
# SAFETY SURFACE: the `JsonValue`/`JsonMember` representations and raw run-copy
# helpers are private to module `Json`; `using Json` preserves the public
# top-level API while preventing callers from fabricating pointer-bearing enum
# variants or calling the raw pointer helpers. The public surface is the
# region-indexed `JsonValueHandle[r]` API: parsing, `json_handle_*` accessors and
# the scalar builders `json_handle_null/bool/number/string`. Array/object-from-
# raw-run constructors are temporarily private until an opaque builder API can
# carry its arena and lifetime safely.
# Views and C strings returned from parsed values still borrow the backing arena;
# resetting/freeing it invalidates them, and this lifetime is not yet enforced by
# the type system.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# What is and is not supported
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# SUPPORTED - the whole of RFC 8259:
#   * null / true / false, and numbers as IEEE-754 doubles, converted with libc
#     `strtod` off an exact NUL-terminated copy of the lexeme (never off the raw
#     input, so `0x10` cannot be mis-read as hex);
#   * strings with every escape the grammar defines - \" \\ \/ \b \f \n \r \t
#     and \uXXXX including surrogate pairs - decoded to UTF-8 at parse time, so
#     `json_as_string` hands back the real bytes, not the source slice;
#   * arrays and objects to a depth of JSON_MAX_DEPTH, nested freely;
#   * the four whitespace bytes (space, tab, CR, LF) anywhere between tokens.
#
# NOT SUPPORTED, on purpose:
#   * any JSON5 / JavaScript extension - comments, trailing commas, unquoted or
#     single-quoted keys, `NaN`, `Infinity`, hex or leading-`+` numbers, leading
#     zeros. All are rejected, all with a position.
#   * LONE SURROGATES. `"\ud800"` with no low half is accepted by CPython's json
#     and by JavaScript; it is rejected here, because a decoded string has to be
#     well-formed UTF-8 and a lone surrogate has no UTF-8 encoding. This is the
#     one place the module is deliberately stricter than the common parsers.
#   * streaming, comments, key sorting, pretty-printing, and any numeric type
#     other than f64 (a 64-bit integer beyond 2^53 loses precision, exactly as
#     it does in JavaScript).
#
# Serialisation is compact (no spaces) and preserves object key INSERTION order,
# duplicates included. Numbers come out as the shortest of the 15/16/17
# significant-digit forms that converts back to the identical double; NaN and
# the infinities, which JSON cannot express, come out as `null`.
#
# REGION STRATEGY: `json_parse` builds each array and object in a `darray` that
# grows as children are appended, and a nested value allocates on top of a
# parent that is still growing. That interleaving needs a CHAINED region - the
# default `region r(N)` form, whose realloc copies. A `region r(N) using
# reserve_commit` (or `using fixed`) grows arrays in place and panics with
# "array cannot grow in place: not the tail allocation" the first time a parent
# has to grow after a child allocated above it. Loud, not silent, but a
# limitation to know about.
#
# Linking: `strtod`/`snprintf` are libc, no extra flag.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Shape notes (why this file is written the way it is)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Five stage0/stage1 differences shaped the code; each has a minimised repro
# under repro/. Read these before "simplifying" anything here.
#
#  1. `JsonValue` is a private, plain, NON-recursive enum: children hang off an opaque
#     `uintptr` + count, never a `JsonValue&`. A `Self&?` payload makes stage1
#     promote the enum to a region-backed packed enum needing an explicit
#     Store, while stage0 keeps it inline.
#     -> repro/nw_json_self_ref_enum_packed_stage1.elisa
#  2. Strings are carried as `(data_bits, len)`, not as an `sview`. An `sview`
#     in an enum-variant payload makes the stage1 backend decline every
#     function that matches on the enum, dropping the unit.
#     -> repro/nw_json_sview_enum_payload_stage1.elisa
#  3. Children are lifted out of a run with `arena_memcpy` (`json_value_copy` /
#     `json_member_copy`), never with `ref[0]`. A function that returns an enum
#     by dereferencing an enum reference is declined by stage1.
#     -> repro/nw_json_enum_deref_return_stage1.elisa
#  4. The recursive descent carries a `JsonErrorCode` in the parser instead of
#     raising: stage1 rejects `try` on a forward-declared callee, so a
#     parse_value <-> parse_array/parse_object pair cannot be spelled at all.
#     -> repro/nw_json_forward_try_error_union_stage1.elisa
#  5. Where `try` is used, its result is bound to a local first. A `try` in
#     call-argument position (`items.push(try parse(...))`) declines on stage1.
#     -> repro/nw_json_try_as_call_argument_stage1.elisa

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# libc externs
# ------------------------------------------------------------

module Json:
    private:
        # The DOM contains raw arena addresses. Keeping its variants and raw run
        # helpers module-private prevents safe callers from fabricating addresses.
        # The arena must still outlive every value and view derived from it.

        @link_name("strtod")
        extern strtod(nptr: u8&, endptr: u8&?) -> f64
        @link_name("snprintf")
        extern snprintf(buffer: u8&?, buffer_size: usize, format: static u8&, ...) -> i32 can[Console.Format]

        # ------------------------------------------------------------
        # Byte constants (the whole JSON alphabet)
        # ------------------------------------------------------------

        const JSON_CH_TAB: i64 = 9
        const JSON_CH_NL: i64 = 10
        const JSON_CH_CR: i64 = 13
        const JSON_CH_SPACE: i64 = 32
        const JSON_CH_DQUOTE: i64 = 34
        const JSON_CH_PLUS: i64 = 43
        const JSON_CH_COMMA: i64 = 44
        const JSON_CH_MINUS: i64 = 45
        const JSON_CH_DOT: i64 = 46
        const JSON_CH_SLASH: i64 = 47
        const JSON_CH_0: i64 = 48
        const JSON_CH_9: i64 = 57
        const JSON_CH_COLON: i64 = 58
        const JSON_CH_UPPER_A: i64 = 65
        const JSON_CH_UPPER_E: i64 = 69
        const JSON_CH_UPPER_F: i64 = 70
        const JSON_CH_LBRACKET: i64 = 91
        const JSON_CH_BSLASH: i64 = 92
        const JSON_CH_RBRACKET: i64 = 93
        const JSON_CH_a: i64 = 97
        const JSON_CH_b: i64 = 98
        const JSON_CH_e: i64 = 101
        const JSON_CH_f: i64 = 102
        const JSON_CH_n: i64 = 110
        const JSON_CH_r: i64 = 114
        const JSON_CH_t: i64 = 116
        const JSON_CH_u: i64 = 117
        const JSON_CH_LBRACE: i64 = 123
        const JSON_CH_RBRACE: i64 = 125

        # Nesting limit. Recursive descent means depth costs host stack, so a hostile
        # document ("[[[[[[...") must be rejected rather than allowed to overflow it.
        const JSON_MAX_DEPTH: i64 = 256
        const JSON_EMPTY_STRING_LENGTH: i64 = 0
        const JSON_NULL_STRING_ADDRESS: uintptr = 0

        # Scratch big enough for any number lexeme worth converting exactly; longer
        # lexemes take the arena path in `json_lexeme_to_f64`.
        const JSON_NUMBER_SCRATCH: i64 = 512

        # ------------------------------------------------------------
        # Value type
        # ------------------------------------------------------------

    public:
        const enum JsonKind of i8:
            NULL = 0
            BOOL = 1
            NUMBER = 2
            STRING = 3
            ARRAY = 4
            OBJECT = 5

    private:
        # A parsed JSON value. `String` carries the DECODED bytes (escapes already
        # resolved, UTF-8), `Array`/`Object` carry the base address of a contiguous
        # arena-allocated run of children plus its length. Empty strings still own one
        # byte (the NUL terminator), so `data_bits` is never 0 for a `String`; empty
        # arrays and objects carry `0.uintptr()` and count 0.
        enum JsonValue:
            Null
            Bool(value: bool)
            Number(value: f64)
            String(data_bits: uintptr, len: i64)
            Array(items_bits: uintptr, count: i64)
            Object(members_bits: uintptr, count: i64)

        # One object entry. Members keep INSERTION ORDER: the parser appends in source
        # order, `json_write` emits in that same order, and duplicate keys are all kept
        # (lookup answers with the first).
        struct JsonMember:
            key_bits: mutable uintptr
            key_len: mutable i64
            value: mutable JsonValue

        # ------------------------------------------------------------
        # Error sets
        # ------------------------------------------------------------

        # Every parse failure carries the byte offset into the input where it was
        # detected, so a caller can point at the offending column.
    public:
        error JsonError:
            UnexpectedEnd(pos: i64)
            UnexpectedChar(pos: i64)
            InvalidLiteral(pos: i64)
            InvalidNumber(pos: i64)
            InvalidString(pos: i64)
            InvalidEscape(pos: i64)
            ExpectedColon(pos: i64)
            ExpectedCommaOrEnd(pos: i64)
            TrailingData(pos: i64)
            DepthExceeded(pos: i64)

        # The same failures as a plain value, one code per `JsonError` variant. The
        # parser threads this rather than an error union - which keeps the recursion
        # free of `try`/`raise` - and `json_parse` turns the code back into the matching
        # variant. `json_parse_checked` hands it to callers directly, which is the only
        # portable way to tell the failures apart today: variant-pattern `catch` arms
        # (`JsonError.InvalidEscape(p):`) are still stage0-only.
        const enum JsonErrorCode of i8:
            OK = 0
            UNEXPECTED_END = 1
            UNEXPECTED_CHAR = 2
            INVALID_LITERAL = 3
            INVALID_NUMBER = 4
            INVALID_STRING = 5
            INVALID_ESCAPE = 6
            EXPECTED_COLON = 7
            EXPECTED_COMMA_OR_END = 8
            TRAILING_DATA = 9
            DEPTH_EXCEEDED = 10

        # Accessor failures are position-free: they are about the value at hand, not
        # about the text it came from.
        error JsonAccessError:
            WrongType
            OutOfRange
            MissingKey

        # ------------------------------------------------------------
        # Parser state
        # ------------------------------------------------------------

    private:
        struct JsonParser:
            text: mutable sview
            pos: mutable i64
            depth: mutable i64
            error_code: mutable JsonErrorCode
            error_pos: mutable i64

        # What `json_parse_checked` reports: `code` is `JsonErrorCode.OK` and `value` is
        # the document on success; otherwise `code`/`pos` say what went wrong and where,
        # and `value` is `JsonValue.Null`.
    public:
        struct JsonParseResult[@r]:
            code: mutable JsonErrorCode
            pos: mutable i64
            value: mutable JsonValueHandle[r]

        # A safe API handle for carrying a parsed node across module boundaries.
        # The payload enum stays private so clients cannot construct raw arena
        # addresses or depend on its representation; use `json_handle_*` below.
        struct JsonValueHandle[@r]:
            private:
                node: JsonValue

        # A decoded string body living in the arena: base address plus byte length,
        # NOT counting the NUL byte that always follows it.

include "./elisacore_json_lex.elisa"
include "./elisacore_json_parse.elisa"
include "./elisacore_json_values.elisa"
include "./elisacore_json_handles.elisa"

using Json
